(DATA STRUCTURES STUDY NOTES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These notes cover the core data structures from a first course. Each section explains what the) Tj T*
(structure stores, which operations it supports, and how fast those operations are.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. MEASURING COST WITH BIG-O) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Big-O notation describes how the running time or memory of an algorithm grows as the input size) Tj T*
(n grows. It ignores constant factors and keeps only the fastest-growing term, so 3n + 20 is) Tj T*
(written O\(n\) and n^2 + n is written O\(n^2\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Common classes, from fastest to slowest: O\(1\) constant time, O\(log n\) logarithmic time, O\(n\)) Tj T*
(linear time, O\(n log n\) linearithmic time, O\(n^2\) quadratic time, and O\(2^n\) exponential time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Amortized cost is the average cost per operation over a long sequence of operations. An) Tj T*
(operation can be expensive once in a while and still have a low amortized cost if the expensive) Tj T*
(cases are rare enough. Appending to a dynamic array is the standard example: most appends are) Tj T*
(O\(1\), an occasional resize is O\(n\), and the amortized cost of an append is O\(1\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. ARRAYS AND DYNAMIC ARRAYS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An array stores elements in one contiguous block of memory. Because every element has the same) Tj T*
(size, the address of element i is the start address plus i times the element size, so reading) Tj T*
(or writing any index takes O\(1\) time. This is called random access.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Inserting or deleting in the middle of an array takes O\(n\) time, because every element after) Tj T*
(that position must shift by one.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A dynamic array grows automatically. When it runs out of space it allocates a new block,) Tj T*
(usually twice as large, and copies every element across. Doubling the capacity is what makes) Tj T*
(the amortized cost of append O\(1\): after a resize to capacity 2c, the next c appends are cheap.) Tj T*
(Growing by a fixed amount instead, such as 10 extra slots each time, makes the amortized append) Tj T*
(cost O\(n\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Arrays have excellent cache locality. Neighbouring elements sit next to each other in memory,) Tj T*
(so the processor loads several of them at once, which makes scanning an array much faster in) Tj T*
(practice than scanning a linked list of the same length.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
