(3. LINKED LISTS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A singly linked list is a chain of nodes. Each node stores a value and a pointer to the next) Tj T*
(node. The list keeps a pointer to the first node, called the head.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Inserting or deleting at the head takes O\(1\) time. Inserting after a node you already hold a) Tj T*
(pointer to also takes O\(1\) time. Finding the element at position i takes O\(n\) time, because you) Tj T*
(must follow i pointers from the head. There is no random access.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A doubly linked list also stores a pointer to the previous node in each node. This allows O\(1\)) Tj T*
(deletion of a node when you hold a pointer to it, and traversal in both directions, at the cost) Tj T*
(of one extra pointer per node.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A common trick for detecting a cycle in a linked list is Floyd's tortoise and hare algorithm:) Tj T*
(one pointer moves one step at a time and another moves two steps at a time. If the list has a) Tj T*
(cycle, the fast pointer eventually meets the slow pointer. It uses O\(1\) extra memory.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. STACKS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A stack is a last-in, first-out \(LIFO\) collection: the most recently added element is the first) Tj T*
(one removed. The two main operations are push, which adds an element to the top, and pop, which) Tj T*
(removes the top element. Peek returns the top element without removing it. All three take O\(1\)) Tj T*
(time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A stack can be implemented with a dynamic array, pushing and popping at the end, or with a) Tj T*
(linked list, pushing and popping at the head.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Uses of stacks include the function call stack, undo history in editors, checking balanced) Tj T*
(parentheses, evaluating postfix expressions, and depth-first search. To check balanced) Tj T*
(brackets, push every opening bracket; on a closing bracket, pop and check that it matches. The) Tj T*
(string is balanced if every pop matches and the stack is empty at the end.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. QUEUES AND DEQUES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A queue is a first-in, first-out \(FIFO\) collection: elements leave in the same order they) Tj T*
(arrived. Enqueue adds an element at the back and dequeue removes the element at the front. Both) Tj T*
(take O\(1\) time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A circular buffer implements a queue with a fixed-size array and two indices, head and tail,) Tj T*
(that wrap around to the start of the array when they reach the end. This avoids shifting) Tj T*
(elements when the front element is removed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A deque, or double-ended queue, allows adding and removing at both ends in O\(1\) time. Queues) Tj T*
(are used for breadth-first search, task scheduling, and buffering data between a producer and a) Tj T*
(consumer.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A priority queue is different: it always removes the element with the highest priority, not the) Tj T*
(oldest one. It is usually implemented with a heap, covered in section 9.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
