(6. HASH TABLES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A hash table stores key-value pairs and supports insert, lookup and delete in O\(1\) average) Tj T*
(time. A hash function turns each key into an integer, and that integer modulo the table size) Tj T*
(gives the bucket where the pair is stored.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A collision happens when two different keys map to the same bucket. Separate chaining handles) Tj T*
(collisions by keeping a small list of entries in each bucket. Open addressing handles) Tj T*
(collisions by probing other buckets in a fixed order until a free one is found; linear probing) Tj T*
(checks the next bucket, then the one after, and so on.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The load factor is the number of stored entries divided by the number of buckets. As the load) Tj T*
(factor grows, collisions become more frequent and operations slow down. In these notes, a table) Tj T*
(is resized to twice as many buckets when the load factor exceeds 0.75, and every entry is) Tj T*
(rehashed into the new table. With a bad hash function, or an attacker choosing keys, every key) Tj T*
(can land in the same bucket and operations degrade to O\(n\) in the worst case.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Hash tables do not keep keys in sorted order. If you need the smallest key or keys in a range,) Tj T*
(use a balanced binary search tree instead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(7. BINARY SEARCH TREES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A binary search tree \(BST\) is a binary tree where every node's key is greater than all keys in) Tj T*
(its left subtree and smaller than all keys in its right subtree. This ordering property lets) Tj T*
(search discard half of the remaining tree at each step when the tree is balanced.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Search, insert and delete take O\(h\) time, where h is the height of the tree. In a balanced tree) Tj T*
(h is O\(log n\). If keys are inserted in sorted order, each new key becomes the right child of) Tj T*
(the previous one, the tree degenerates into a linked list, and h becomes n, so operations take) Tj T*
(O\(n\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An in-order traversal of a BST visits left subtree, node, right subtree, and outputs the keys) Tj T*
(in sorted order. Pre-order visits the node before its subtrees; post-order visits it after.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Deleting a node with two children replaces its key with its in-order successor, which is the) Tj T*
(smallest key in its right subtree, and then deletes that successor node, which has at most one) Tj T*
(child.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
