(8. BALANCED TREES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An AVL tree is a self-balancing BST. For every node, the heights of its left and right subtrees) Tj T*
(differ by at most one. After an insert or delete, the tree checks this balance factor on the) Tj T*
(path back to the root and restores it with rotations. A single rotation fixes a left-left or) Tj T*
(right-right imbalance; a double rotation fixes a left-right or right-left imbalance. Height) Tj T*
(stays O\(log n\), so search, insert and delete are all O\(log n\) in the worst case.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A red-black tree is another self-balancing BST. It colours each node red or black and enforces) Tj T*
(rules that keep the longest root-to-leaf path at most twice the length of the shortest one.) Tj T*
(Red-black trees do fewer rotations than AVL trees on insert and delete, while AVL trees are) Tj T*
(more strictly balanced and slightly faster to search.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A B-tree is a balanced tree whose nodes hold many keys and have many children. Because each) Tj T*
(node is large, the tree is very shallow, which means few disk reads per lookup. Databases and) Tj T*
(file systems use B-trees for their indexes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(9. HEAPS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A binary heap is a complete binary tree stored in an array. In a min-heap every parent is) Tj T*
(smaller than or equal to its children, so the minimum is always at the root. For the node at) Tj T*
(index i, its children are at indices 2i + 1 and 2i + 2, and its parent is at index \(i - 1\) / 2) Tj T*
(rounded down.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Insert places the new element at the end of the array and sifts it up, swapping with its parent) Tj T*
(while it is smaller. Extract-min removes the root, moves the last element to the root, and) Tj T*
(sifts it down, swapping with the smaller child. Both take O\(log n\) time. Reading the minimum) Tj T*
(takes O\(1\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Building a heap from n unsorted elements by sifting down every non-leaf node from the bottom up) Tj T*
(takes O\(n\) time, which is faster than n separate inserts at O\(n log n\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Heapsort builds a max-heap and repeatedly moves the maximum to the end of the array. It runs in) Tj T*
(O\(n log n\) time in every case and uses O\(1\) extra memory, but it is not stable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(10. GRAPHS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A graph is a set of vertices connected by edges. Edges can be directed or undirected, and) Tj T*
(weighted or unweighted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An adjacency matrix stores a V by V table where entry \(u, v\) says whether an edge exists.) Tj T*
(Checking one edge takes O\(1\) time but the matrix uses O\(V^2\) memory, which wastes space for) Tj T*
(sparse graphs. An adjacency list stores, for every vertex, a list of its neighbours. It uses) Tj T*
(O\(V + E\) memory and is the usual choice for sparse graphs.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Breadth-first search \(BFS\) explores the graph level by level using a queue. In an unweighted) Tj T*
(graph, BFS finds the shortest path, measured in number of edges, from the start vertex to every) Tj T*
(other vertex. Depth-first search \(DFS\) goes as deep as possible along each branch before) Tj T*
(backtracking, using a stack or recursion. DFS is used for cycle detection, topological sorting,) Tj T*
(and finding connected components. Both BFS and DFS take O\(V + E\) time with an adjacency list.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A topological sort orders the vertices of a directed acyclic graph so that every edge goes from) Tj T*
(an earlier vertex to a later one. It exists only if the graph has no cycles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
