(11. SHORTEST PATHS AND SPANNING TREES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dijkstra's algorithm finds the shortest paths from one source vertex in a graph with) Tj T*
(non-negative edge weights. It keeps a priority queue of vertices ordered by their current best) Tj T*
(distance, repeatedly removes the closest unfinished vertex, and relaxes its outgoing edges.) Tj T*
(With a binary heap it runs in O\(\(V + E\) log V\) time. Dijkstra's algorithm gives wrong answers) Tj T*
(when some edge weights are negative.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Bellman-Ford algorithm handles negative edge weights. It relaxes every edge V - 1 times,) Tj T*
(which takes O\(V E\) time, and one more round of relaxation detects a negative cycle if any) Tj T*
(distance still improves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A minimum spanning tree connects all vertices of a weighted undirected graph with the smallest) Tj T*
(total edge weight. Kruskal's algorithm sorts the edges by weight and adds each edge that does) Tj T*
(not create a cycle, using a union-find structure to check for cycles. Prim's algorithm grows) Tj T*
(the tree from one vertex, always adding the cheapest edge that reaches a new vertex.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(12. SORTING) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A sorting algorithm is stable if equal elements keep their original relative order. Stability) Tj T*
(matters when sorting records by one field after they were already sorted by another.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Merge sort splits the array in half, sorts each half recursively, and merges the two sorted) Tj T*
(halves. It takes O\(n log n\) time in every case, is stable, and needs O\(n\) extra memory for) Tj T*
(merging.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Quicksort picks a pivot, partitions the array into elements smaller and larger than the pivot,) Tj T*
(and sorts each part recursively. Its average time is O\(n log n\) but its worst case is O\(n^2\),) Tj T*
(which happens when the pivot is always the smallest or largest element, for example when the) Tj T*
(first element is chosen as pivot on an already sorted array. Choosing a random pivot makes the) Tj T*
(worst case very unlikely. Quicksort sorts in place and is usually the fastest comparison sort) Tj T*
(in practice, but it is not stable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Insertion sort takes O\(n^2\) time in general but O\(n\) on nearly sorted input, and it is fast on) Tj T*
(very small arrays, so practical sorting libraries switch to insertion sort for small subarrays.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(No comparison-based sort can beat O\(n log n\) in the worst case. Counting sort and radix sort) Tj T*
(avoid comparisons and can sort integers in linear time when the range of values is limited.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
